    @section ncursestoolsqrepl Full-Screen Qore REPL

    The installed \c qrepl command provides an interactive %Qore console with menus, a file
    browser, captured output, history, and a scrollable output pane. Start it in a terminal:
    @verbatim
qrepl --help
qrepl
    @endverbatim

    Enter evaluates the draft; Alt-Enter inserts a hard newline. Up/Down navigate
    history for a single-line draft and move visually within multiline input.
    Ctrl-P/N always navigate history; Ctrl-R opens a literal history search.
    The unfinished draft and selection return after moving past the newest entry.
    Errors retain the draft. Ctrl-Z/Y undo/redo; Shift-arrows select text.
    Ctrl-C/X/V use an application-local clipboard shared with transcript selection.
    This does not modify the desktop clipboard. Ctrl-A/E move to the hard-line
    boundaries; Ctrl-K/U delete toward a hard-line boundary; Ctrl-W deletes a word.

    PageUp/PageDown scroll output independently of the draft. Scrolling up pauses
    following; the status bar reports unread lines. Ctrl-End or View > Latest Output
    resumes following. F6 switches focus between draft and output; focused output
    supports arrows/Home/End, Shift-click selection and Ctrl-C copy. Ctrl-S or
    View > Search Output opens literal search. F3/Shift-F3 visit next/previous matches.
    Bracketed paste inserts one undoable operation without evaluating embedded newlines.
    Output retains the latest 10,000 logical lines; the editor grows to six rows and
    then scrolls within its allocation. Syntax highlighting uses the optional astparser
    module. Tab completion uses the live session, replaces the full token around the
    caret, and preserves existing parentheses or namespace separators. The popup
    provides documentation and signatures; Tab switches to its help pane. Escape
    dismisses it, and an accepted completion is one undoable edit.

    Use F1 for keyboard help and <tt>/help</tt> for console commands. Ctrl-Q, Alt-Q,
    File > Quit and typed quit commands use the same confirmation. Alt-F opens File;
    Ctrl-Left/Right move by word. File > Load opens the browser; File > Save asks for
    a filename and confirms replacement of an existing file. Cancel restores the draft.
    Paused/unread output and execution state take priority over secondary status text.
    Alt-key menu shortcuts provide file, edit, view and help actions. Command history
    is persisted in <tt>~/.qore-repl-history</tt>. Output capture preserves complete
    lines and marks unterminated output with a reversed percent sign. Capture workers
    enqueue text only; widget mutation and drawing stay on the UI thread. Each worker
    drain uses a finite byte-count snapshot, so continuously produced output cannot
    starve flush or shutdown. Ordinary Enter remains LF even with paste enabled.

    Evaluation runs in one worker. Typing, output navigation, searching and resizing
    stay available while it executes; duplicate submissions and backend assistance are
    held until completion. Edits made during evaluation are not cleared by its result.
    Quit during execution offers to finish and then exit; it does not claim to cancel
    arbitrary Qore code. Output arrives through a capture reader and wakes the UI.

    @section ncursestoolscontrollers Reusable UI Workflows

    @ref NcursesUi::EditorAssistance owns versioned results, popup placement, dismissal
    and focus restoration. @ref NcursesQore::CodeSupport adapts an existing QoreRepl
    evaluator; NcursesUi itself has no Qore language dependency. Keep evaluator calls
    serialized with assistance, while syntax highlighting uses a separate parser.
    This complete, headless example checks an atomic completion and undo:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses
%requires NcursesUi
%requires NcursesQore
%requires QoreRepl
Ncurses::TestTerminal terminal(12, 60);
on_exit terminal.close();
NcursesUi::Application app({"test_terminal": terminal});
on_exit app.close();
NcursesUi::TextEditorWidget editor();
app.setRoot(editor);
app.getFocusManager().setFocus(editor);
QoreRepl::QoreRepl repl({"history_file": NOTHING});
NcursesQore::CodeSupport language(repl);
editor.setHighlighter(string sub (string source) { return language.highlight(source); });
NcursesUi::EditorAssistance help(app, editor,
    hash<NcursesUi::AssistanceResponse> sub (hash<NcursesUi::AssistanceRequest> request) {
        return language.complete(request);
    });
on_exit help.close();
editor.setText("get_qore_library_in");
@assert(help.request(True));
@assert(editor.getText() == "get_qore_library_info()");
@assert(editor.getBuffer().undo());
@assert(editor.getText() == "get_qore_library_in");
    @endcode

    @ref NcursesUi::BackgroundTask owns single-worker execution, ActionModel state,
    UI-thread completion delivery and teardown. Its start(work, finished) rejects a
    second start until the first completion is delivered. The work callback must not
    access widgets. join() is a deterministic test/shutdown barrier; interactive event
    handlers must not call it. close() releases callbacks and joins without claiming
    to cancel the operation. Applications may retain the model for progress views.
    If queue publication fails, a bounded recovery slot retains the failure and wakes
    the event loop; the next UI turn reports a failed outcome and permits retry.
    Embedders without Application::run() call BackgroundTask::processPending() after
    Application::processPending(). join() surfaces an undelivered failure if the UI
    cannot run, for example after the terminal has closed.

    @ref NcursesUi::Dialogs provides confirm(), message(), prompt() and openFile().
    Prompt fields reuse FormWidget labels, descriptions, validation, mouse focus and
    scrolling. Callback semantics stay in the application; close() releases all owned
    panels. PanelWidget::setCenteredSize() retains preferred dimensions across resize
    while immediately clamping to the terminal. StatusBarWidget segment priority and
    compact labels keep essential state visible; higher priorities are allocated first,
    while display order and alignment remain unchanged. Legacy segments retain clipping.

    @section ncursestoolshelpers Building a Custom Console

    @ref ncursesrepluiintro "NcursesReplUi" provides editing primitives independently of the
    full-screen program. @ref NcursesReplUi::InputBuffer "InputBuffer" tracks text and cursor
    position, while @ref QoreHistory::History "History" stores commands. The module reexports
    the history API. This example edits a command and saves it without opening a terminal:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires NcursesReplUi

NcursesReplUi::InputBuffer buffer();
buffer.insertChar("1 + 2");
buffer.moveHome();
buffer.insertChar("print ");
@assert(buffer.getText() == "print 1 + 2");
QoreHistory::History history();
history.add(buffer.getText());
@assert(history.prev() == "print 1 + 2");
hash<auto> layout = NcursesReplUi::LayoutCalc::calcLayout(24, 80);
@assert(layout.output_rows == 21);
    @endcode

    @ref NcursesReplUi::LayoutCalc::calcLayout() "LayoutCalc::calcLayout()" divides terminal
    dimensions into output, input, and information panes;
    @ref NcursesReplUi::ScrollCalc "ScrollCalc" computes bottom-anchored scrolling positions.
    Pair these helpers with @ref NcursesUi::ScrollableWidget "ScrollableWidget" and
    @ref NcursesUi::StatusBarWidget "StatusBarWidget" as described in
    @ref ncurses_ui_widgetsguide "UI Widget Toolkit".
*/
